WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Why does fast population growth bend instead of climbing forever?

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.960
Watch a Chesapeake creek as the blue crab population changes.

3
00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.900
The water keeps moving, but food and hiding places are limited.

4
00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.880
Predators and disease also affect survival.

5
00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.780
Something in this picture must push back as the population grows.

6
00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:23.780
... ...

7
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:31.720
A population is all the members of one species living in the same area at the same

8
00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.140
time. Teal crab symbols track its size.

9
00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.840
Gold food and green shelter show resources.

10
00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.920
Red arrows show living challenges, such as predators and disease.

11
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.960
A pale blue band shows cold water, a nonliving challenge.

12
00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:53.680
Together, these living and nonliving factors can limit growth.

13
00:00:54.783 --> 00:00:58.163
Start with a small population and plentiful resources.

14
00:00:59.363 --> 00:01:04.323
The population increases by the same fraction each step, so the number added grows.

15
00:01:05.683 --> 00:01:07.643
That pattern is exponential growth.

16
00:01:08.823 --> 00:01:14.903
Its computed curve bends upward like a J. This model is schematic, but the curve

17
00:01:14.903 --> 00:01:16.203
follows its stated rule.

18
00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:19.527
Now predict what makes this curve bend.

19
00:01:20.947 --> 00:01:22.927
Must the food and shelter vanish completely?

20
00:01:24.087 --> 00:01:26.747
Or can crowding strengthen limits before they are gone?

21
00:01:28.047 --> 00:01:30.447
Keep your answer while the creek continues moving.

22
00:01:36.533 --> 00:01:40.013
Crowding means more crabs share the same food and shelter.

23
00:01:41.833 --> 00:01:42.833
Competition grows.

24
00:01:43.853 --> 00:01:47.473
Disease may spread more easily, and predators may find more prey.

25
00:01:48.873 --> 00:01:51.413
Fewer young survive for each crab already present.

26
00:01:52.673 --> 00:01:57.173
The population can still grow, but its growth slows before every resource

27
00:01:57.173 --> 00:01:58.173
disappears.

28
00:01:58.917 --> 00:02:05.057
In the simplest limited-growth model, that slowing turns the J into an S. The

29
00:02:05.057 --> 00:02:07.597
computed line approaches carrying capacity.

30
00:02:08.977 --> 00:02:14.337
Carrying capacity is the largest population this environment can support over time,

31
00:02:14.697 --> 00:02:16.677
with its resources and challenges.

32
00:02:17.997 --> 00:02:20.997
It is a level approached, not a wall that nothing crosses.

33
00:02:22.217 --> 00:02:24.797
Read the S-shaped curve from beginning to end.

34
00:02:26.317 --> 00:02:29.237
Growth starts slowly because few individuals can reproduce.

35
00:02:30.597 --> 00:02:33.797
Then numbers rise faster while resources are plentiful.

36
00:02:35.237 --> 00:02:37.917
Finally, growth slows near carrying capacity.

37
00:02:39.137 --> 00:02:43.057
With little movement in or out, births and deaths are about equal there.

38
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.520
The computed line simply approaches carrying capacity.

39
00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:52.460
In real populations, births, deaths and movement continue.

40
00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:57.540
When that balance shifts, size can move up and down around the level.

41
00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:02.820
That balance, kept by ongoing change, is dynamic equilibrium.

42
00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:07.320
Real populations may also overshoot or decline.

43
00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.200
The S shape is a useful model, not a required life story.

44
00:03:13.333 --> 00:03:14.613
Return to the creek.

45
00:03:16.013 --> 00:03:20.653
More crabs made the same resources harder to share, and several challenges grew

46
00:03:20.653 --> 00:03:25.333
stronger. Those limits reduced growth before the creek was empty.

47
00:03:26.553 --> 00:03:27.893
That is why the curve bent.

48
00:03:29.213 --> 00:03:32.393
The next question is whether the support level itself can move.
